  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🍀 Halfling Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Homely given names, family names that began as nicknames, and nicknames that might yet stick."
    },
    "intro": "Halfling names sound like the people: warm, short and a little bit cheeky. In D&D a halfling has a given name, a family name and possibly a nickname, and the family name is often a nickname that stuck so well it was handed down. This **halfling name generator** builds all three, so you can meet Hetty Kettlewhistle, or her cousin Pip “Butterfingers” Puddlefoot.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Given name, family name, nickname",
        "content": "<p>The fifth-edition Player's Handbook (2014) describes halfling names in three parts:</p><ul><li><strong>A given name.</strong> Short and friendly, often the kind of name you'd find in an English village.</li><li><strong>A family name.</strong> Often a nickname that stuck so tenaciously it was passed down the generations: a great-grandmother who was quick on her feet becomes the Quickstep family.</li><li><strong>A nickname</strong>, which halflings may or may not have. It's earned, usually by something they did or keep doing.</li></ul><p>The book also splits halflings into lightfoot (sociable wanderers) and stout (hardier, said to have dwarven blood). Both use the same kind of names.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "What makes a family name sound halfling",
        "content": "<p>Halfling family names are compound words built from home and countryside:</p><ul><li><strong>Food and drink:</strong> Honeypot, Oakbarrel, Teapot.</li><li><strong>Plants and fields:</strong> Thistledown, Bramblewick, Wheatfield.</li><li><strong>Water and land:</strong> Fernbrook, Willowbank, Riverbend.</li><li><strong>Feet and moving about:</strong> Barleyfoot, Puddlefoot, Quickstep.</li></ul><p>Say the name and picture the ancestor it came from. If the picture makes you smile, it's a halfling name.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Halflings and Tolkien's hobbits",
        "content": "<p>D&D's halflings began as a stand-in for Tolkien's <a href=\"hobbit-name-generator.html\">hobbits</a>, and the names still show it. Tolkien gave hobbits family names like Baggins, Took, Brandybuck and Proudfoot, and hobbit girls were often named after flowers or jewels: Belladonna, Lobelia, Primula.</p><p>This generator's female names lean the same way (Marigold, Poppy, Primrose) without using Tolkien's own characters. For names from Middle-earth itself, see the <a href=\"lotr-wizard-name-generator.html\">LOTR Wizard</a> and <a href=\"lotr-orc-name-generator.html\">LOTR Orc</a> generators, or the Tolkien style in the <a href=\"elf-name-generator.html\">Elf Name Generator</a>.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Picking a nickname",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Make it about something they did.</strong> \"Two-Pies\" has a story behind it, and someone at the table will ask.</li><li><strong>Let it be a bit unfair.</strong> Nicknames are given by others. A careful rogue called \"Butterfingers\" has a reason to prove everyone wrong.</li><li><strong>Watch it become a family name.</strong> If your halfling becomes famous, their grandchildren might carry the nickname as a surname, the way the book describes.</li></ul>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "How do halfling names work in D&D?",
        "answer": "A halfling has a given name, a family name and sometimes a nickname. In the fifth-edition Player's Handbook, family names are often old nicknames that stuck so well they were passed down."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are halflings the same as hobbits?",
        "answer": "D&D's halflings were inspired by Tolkien's hobbits, so the names sound alike, but halflings are D&D's own people with their own lore, such as the lightfoot and stout halflings."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are good halfling last names?",
        "answer": "Compound words from home and countryside: Thistledown, Kettlewhistle, Puddlefoot, Honeypot. Set Name Format to \"Family name only\" to see a list."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are these names from the Player's Handbook?",
        "answer": "No. They're new names in the same style, though a few given names, like Bree and Daisy, are ordinary English names that may appear elsewhere."
      }
    ]
  }
